<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apennines</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Apennine Mountains</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apennines are the mountain range that runs about 1,200 kilometres down the length of the Italian peninsula, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a>. Their valleys and uplands were home to many ancient Italic peoples, such as the Samnites, Sabines and Umbrians, and their passes linked the Tyrrhenian and Adriatic sides of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. The highest peak is Corno Grande in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-sasso">Gran Sasso</a> massif.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/apennines.jpg" alt="Apennines" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Luciodem assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_Gran_Sasso_d%27Italia,_il_paretone_nord.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
